Subject: winex11: preserve precision scrolling from XInput2 scroll valuators

XInput2 2.1 reports touchpad and high-resolution wheel scrolling as
cumulative valuator positions. One increment stands for one wheel
notch. Wine consumes only the legacy button events the X server
synthesizes from those valuators, so every update collapses to a whole
WHEEL_DELTA and two-finger navigation is visibly stepped.

The driver selects XI_Motion, XI_ButtonPress and XI_ButtonRelease on
each top-level window whose Wine window policy takes pointer events. A
WS_EX_TRANSPARENT window lets clicks fall through (see
get_window_attributes), and an XI selection would make it a delivery
target again, so the selection follows the same style bit instead of
asking the X server with a synchronous XGetWindowAttributes call. The
driver registers the window context before the selection, so the
selection can read the policy at creation. SmoothScrolling=disabled
keeps Wine's core button path. SmoothScrolling=notched floors each
report to whole notches and carries the remainder, for an application
that ignores fractional wheel units.

Scroll-valuator changes become fractional vertical and horizontal
wheel input, and the driver carries the sub-unit remainder.
WHEEL_DELTA is a unit, not a minimum: an application that follows the
amount rather than counting messages scrolls continuously. The wheel
input carries no position. The server stamps each message at the
current cursor, which is where X delivered the event, so wheel
delivery never moves the cursor. Both wheel paths update the window
user time, at most once per 100 ms: the property write wakes the X
server and the window manager, and a scroll stream reports hundreds of
times a second. Focus-stealing prevention still tracks scrolling as
user activity.

The driver delivers scrolling whether or not a button is held.
WM_MOUSEWHEEL defines MK_LBUTTON and friends as ordinary states of a
wheel message, and dropping input while a button is down would lose
high-resolution wheel movement during drags and pointing-stick
scrolling.

The valuators are cumulative and advance whenever the device scrolls
anywhere, including in other applications and other Wine threads'
windows, so a cached baseline stays meaningful only while this thread
observes the stream continuously. The driver invalidates and reseeds
the baselines, reporting no movement, on window enter, focus in and
out, cursor-clip grabs, device change, and device removal. XInput2
reuses device IDs after removal, so a removed device also loses its
cache entry; the driver selects hierarchy events for exactly that. A
report past the 16-notch cap advances the baseline all the way and
discards the excess, so no debt survives to burst into a later report.
A jump beyond any plausible single report reseeds instead of
scrolling: the server may legally reset a cumulative valuator.

The driver pre-queries each slave device's scroll axes once at thread
init, so the first scroll of a session pays no XIQueryDevice round
trip. It queries a late-arriving device on that device's first report.
A full cache evicts the device that reported longest ago.

The driver ignores the emulated XI scroll button duplicates; their
reports only refresh the baselines. Native XI wheel buttons from
devices without scroll axes still deliver whole notches. The driver
leaves core wheel button events alone: during a core grab (popup menu
tracking, interactive move/size, ClipCursor) the server delivers only
core events, and dropping them would kill wheel scrolling entirely.

Selecting a master-device XI event stops the X server from delivering
its core equivalent. Non-scroll XI motion and button events forward
through Wine's existing core handlers, including button state
reconstruction, so the smooth path preserves ordinary pointer motion,
clicks, activation timestamps and middle-drag handling. A report that
carries pointer motion next to the scroll axes forwards the motion
too.

Builds without XInput2 2.1 scroll definitions compile this path out.
